/*!
    \page cmake-set-variables-and-properties.html
    \title Setting CMake variables and properties
    \brief Describes where to set the CMake variables and properties that Qt
           defines.

    Qt's CMake API is controlled by CMake variables and CMake properties. The
    \l{CMake Variable Reference} and the \l{CMake Property Reference} list which
    ones exist and what they do. This page describes where to set them, and what
    to watch out for when you do.

    \section1 Variables and properties

    A \e{CMake variable} holds a value in the scope of the directory or function
    where it is set. Variables are set with the
    \l{https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/command/set.html}{set()} command.

    A \e{cache variable} is stored in the \c CMakeCache.txt file of the build
    directory and persists across CMake runs. Cache variables are the ones you
    can set from outside the project: on the command line, in a preset, or in an
    IDE.

    A \e property is attached to an entity: a target, a source file, a directory,
    or the global scope. Properties are always set from within the project.

    \section1 Setting variables

    \section2 In the project

    Use the \c set() command to set a variable in the project's
    \c CMakeLists.txt:

    \badcode
    set(QT_I18N_TRANSLATED_LANGUAGES de fr)
    \endcode

    The variable is visible in the current directory scope and in every
    subdirectory that is added afterwards, but not in sibling directories or in
    the parent directory.

    Set the variable \e before the command that reads it. The documentation of a
    variable states when it is read. Some variables are read while the Qt package
    is loaded and have to be set before the first \c{find_package(Qt6)} call.
    \l{cmake-variable-QT_ENABLE_VERBOSE_DEPLOYMENT}{QT_ENABLE_VERBOSE_DEPLOYMENT}
    is one of them:

    \badcode
    cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
    project(MyApp)

    set(QT_ENABLE_VERBOSE_DEPLOYMENT ON)

    find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Widgets)
    qt_standard_project_setup()

    qt_add_executable(MyApp main.cpp)
    \endcode

    A \c set() call without the \c CACHE keyword creates an ordinary variable
    that hides a cache variable of the same name in that scope. If a value is
    meant to be configurable from outside the project, do not overwrite it with
    an unconditional \c set().

    \section2 From outside the project

    Cache variables are set with \c{-D} when you configure the project:

    \badcode
    cmake -DQT_ENABLE_VERBOSE_DEPLOYMENT=ON -S <source-dir> -B <build-dir>
    \endcode

    They can also be set in a \c CMakePresets.json file or in a CMake GUI. None
    of this is specific to Qt. See the
    \l{https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/guide/user-interaction/index.html}
    {CMake User Interaction Guide} for the general mechanics, and
    \l{https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/manual/cmake-presets.7.html}
    {cmake-presets(7)} for the preset file format.

    The \l{Building projects on the command line}{qt-cmake} script forwards all
    of its arguments to CMake, so \c{-D} works there as well:

    \badcode \QtVersion
    C:\Qt\\1\msvc2022_64\bin\qt-cmake -DQT_ENABLE_VERBOSE_DEPLOYMENT=ON -S <source-dir> -B <build-dir>
    \endcode

    \note Changing a cache variable that Qt reads at configure time only takes
    effect after CMake has run again. Build tools such as Ninja re-run CMake
    automatically when \c CMakeCache.txt changes.

    \section2 In an IDE

    In \QC, go to \uicontrol Projects > \uicontrol Build to open the build
    settings of the current kit. The \uicontrol CMake section lists the variables
    in two groups:

    \list
        \li \uicontrol {Initial Configuration} holds the variables that \QC
            passes to CMake when it configures the build directory from scratch,
            such as \c CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH.
        \li \uicontrol {Current Configuration} shows the current contents of
            \c CMakeCache.txt.
    \endlist

    Both groups have a \uicontrol {Batch Edit} button that lets you enter several
    \c{-D} arguments at once, in the same syntax you would use on the command
    line. \QC re-runs CMake when you apply the changes. For details, see
    \l{\QC: CMake Build Configuration},
    \l{\QC: Override CMake settings for a project}, and \l{\QC: CMake Presets}.

    In Visual Studio Code, the CMake Tools extension reads presets, and takes
    individual variables from the \c cmake.configureSettings object in
    \c{.vscode/settings.json}. See \l{\QVSC Documentation} for how to set up Qt
    in Visual Studio Code. Visual Studio reads \c CMakePresets.json, or
    \c CMakeSettings.json if no presets file exists.

    \section1 Setting properties

    Properties cannot be set with \c{-D}. They are always set from the project,
    with the \l{https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/command/set_property.html}
    {set_property()} command or one of its convenience wrappers.

    \section2 Target properties

    Set target properties after the target has been created, and before the
    command that reads them:

    \badcode
    qt_add_executable(MyApp main.cpp)

    set_target_properties(MyApp PROPERTIES
        QT_RESOURCE_PREFIX "/myapp"
    )

    qt_add_resources(MyApp "images" FILES logo.png)
    \endcode

    The \c set_property() command is equivalent and can append to a property that
    holds a list:

    \badcode
    set_property(TARGET MyApp PROPERTY QT_ANDROID_EXTRA_LIBS "${extra_lib}")
    set_property(TARGET MyApp APPEND PROPERTY QT_ANDROID_EXTRA_LIBS "${another_lib}")
    \endcode

    Properties that Qt reads during target finalization, such as the Android
    packaging properties, must be set before \l qt_finalize_target() runs. If you
    do not call \l qt_finalize_target() yourself, finalization happens at the end
    of the directory scope, so setting the property anywhere in the same
    \c CMakeLists.txt is sufficient.

    \section2 Source file properties

    Use \c set_source_files_properties():

    \badcode
    set_source_files_properties(images/logo.png PROPERTIES
        QT_RESOURCE_ALIAS "logo.png"
    )

    qt_add_resources(MyApp "images" FILES images/logo.png)
    \endcode

    \note Source file properties are only visible in the directory scope in which
    they are set. If the file is added to a target in a different directory, pass
    \c TARGET_DIRECTORY or \c DIRECTORY to \c set_source_files_properties() to
    also set the property in that scope. Both options require CMake 3.18 or
    newer.

    \section2 Directory properties

    Directory properties apply to the current directory scope and are inherited
    by subdirectories that are added afterwards:

    \badcode
    set_directory_properties(PROPERTIES QT_EXCLUDE_FROM_TRANSLATION ON)
    \endcode

    \section2 Global properties

    Global properties apply to the whole build:

    \badcode
    set_property(GLOBAL PROPERTY QT_TARGETS_FOLDER "MyQtTargets")
    \endcode

    \section2 Making a property configurable from outside the project

    To control a property from the command line or an IDE, route it through a
    cache variable:

    \badcode
    set(MY_APP_ANDROID_PACKAGE_NAME "org.example.myapp"
        CACHE STRING "Android package name of MyApp")

    set_target_properties(MyApp PROPERTIES
        QT_ANDROID_PACKAGE_NAME "${MY_APP_ANDROID_PACKAGE_NAME}"
    )
    \endcode

    The property can then be changed with:

    \badcode
    cmake -DMY_APP_ANDROID_PACKAGE_NAME=org.example.myapp.beta -S <source-dir> -B <build-dir>
    \endcode

    \sa {CMake Variable Reference}, {CMake Property Reference},
        {Building projects on the command line}
*/
